# Everything this app can be told, and what happens when it is not told it.
# Nothing here is required to run the cellar: every variable below is a
# capability that switches on when set and degrades honestly when absent.

# The database. The only genuinely required one.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname"

# Claude, for Scan, Suggest and Research. Without it those three features
# report that they are unavailable (lib/ai-errors.js) and the rest of the
# cellar works normally.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""

# Photo storage, for scanned labels. Without it scanning still works and
# simply keeps no picture.
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=""

# --- Accounts (Phase 1 of separate cellars per user) ---------------------
#
# AUTH_SECRET plus at least one provider's own pair must be set together.
# Until then the app runs exactly as it always has: one owner, no sign-in,
# every page open. That is a deliberate state - it means this can be
# deployed and watched before it changes anything, and a half-configured
# deploy cannot lock the owner out of their own cellar.
#
# Two sign-in doors exist, and they are independent - set up either one
# alone, or both. Google needs a Google account; email needs none, just an
# inbox, which is the one to set up for anyone invited who doesn't have or
# want a Google account.
#
# AUTH_SECRET signs the session cookie. Generate one with:
#   npx auth secret
AUTH_SECRET=""

# --- Door 1: Google --------------------------------------------------
#
# From Google Cloud Console -> APIs & Services -> Credentials ->
# Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application.
#
# Authorised redirect URIs must include, exactly:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
#   https://YOUR-DOMAIN/api/auth/callback/google
#
# Google will also ask you to configure the OAuth consent screen first.
# "External" with the app left in Testing is the right choice for an
# invite-only app used by a handful of people, and can stay that way
# indefinitely: Testing's usual 7-day token expiry only applies when an app
# requests more than name/email/profile, which this one never does. Add
# each person's Google address as a Test user there as well as inviting
# them in the app - Google refuses anyone not on that list before this app
# ever sees them, regardless of the invite list.
#
# Creating the consent screen and the client ID does not require billing on
# the project; a "start your free trial" prompt Google shows separately, if
# it appears, can be skipped.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

# --- Door 2: email (magic link, via Resend) ---------------------------
#
# No Google account, no password - a one-time link sent to an inbox,
# good for 24 hours. From resend.com/api-keys.
AUTH_RESEND_KEY=""

# Who the link appears to come from. Cannot be left to Resend's own
# default sender, and cannot be any address you like: Resend's shared
# testing address (onboarding@resend.dev) only delivers to the Resend
# account's own owner, so it can prove the flow works but cannot actually
# invite anyone else. Reaching real invitees needs a domain verified in
# Resend (resend.com/domains - a few DNS records at whatever domain you
# already control) and an address at that domain, e.g.
# "Cellarmaster <signin@your-domain.com>".
#
# Free tier: 3,000 emails/month, 100/day - vastly more than an invite-only
# handful of people will ever use.
AUTH_RESEND_FROM=""

# Only needed when NOT deploying to Vercel. Auth.js trusts the request host
# automatically when it sees Vercel's own environment (or when running in
# development); anywhere else in production it refuses with UntrustedHost
# and every auth endpoint returns 500. Set this to "true", or set AUTH_URL
# to the app's public origin.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST=""

# Optional, and worth setting for exactly one deploy. The cellar that
# already exists is unclaimed until somebody signs in, and the first
# allowed sign-in adopts it. Setting this to your own Google address means
# only you can be that person, which turns "whoever gets there first
# inherits every bottle" into "only I do". It does nothing once claimed.
#
# Must match the address Google actually returns, exactly - not whatever
# dotted spelling you're used to typing. Gmail ignores dots for routing
# mail (andrew.rucker@ and andrewrucker@ reach the same inbox), but the
# account has one canonical spelling, and that is what the OAuth profile
# hands back. A mismatch here reads as an ordinary AccessDenied with
# nothing in the code to suggest why - if the very first sign-in gets
# refused, check the account's real address before anything else. The
# safest way to get it right is to attempt the sign-in once, unclaimed,
# and read the address back off the AccessDenied - not to guess the
# canonical form up front.
OWNER_EMAIL=""
